<p>What does this tell us? Well, we know that the p-value of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?0.66"> is the probability of observing a value at least as extreme as what we observed, if our assumption of the data following a uniform distribution was true. This is quite a high probability, which reflects perhaps that our data is not very extreme at all.</p>
<blockquote class="blockquote">
<p>We may also recall that the mean of the chi-squared distribution is equal to its degrees of freedom. In this case, we have 49 degrees of freedom, and it’s intuitive to reason that an observed value of 44.42 is not that extreme when compared to the mean of 49 after all.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This calls into question Dr John’s claim that our observed data is “unusual” when comparing to what’s expected from a uniform distribution.</p>
<section id="how-did-dr-john-arrive-at-his-expected-value" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="how-did-dr-john-arrive-at-his-expected-value">How did Dr John arrive at his expected value?</h3>
<p>Earlier, Dr John said that the number of counts of 5 we should expect to have seen, assuming the data followed a uniform distribution, was 8.89. Considering he did not back up his reasoning, where could he have possibly gotten this value from?</p>


